sed: 2, B5 used: 2. Unshown B4:2, B5:2 \u2014 possible.\nBut this implies we are holding extra B4 and B5 that are not part of the meld. That suggests the meld [B4,B5,B6] is **not using our own B4/B5**, which contradicts standard rules \u2014 when you chi, you use your own two tiles and the discarded one. So if we formed [B4,B5,B6] via chi, we must have contributed two of them.\n\nGiven history: \"Chi B5\" appears in our history \u2192 means we performed 'Chi' on a discarded B5 to complete a chow. To do [B4,B5,B6], we would need to have had B4 and B6 in hand. Then after chi, we discard B7 \u2014 yes, \"Play B7\" follows \"Chi B5\".\n\nSo after Chi B5: we used B4 and B6 from hand + discarded B5 to form [B4,B5,B6]. Therefore, we should **no longer have B4 or B6** in hand.\n\nBut current hand says: [W3:2; B3:1; B4:1; B5:1] \u2014 contradiction.\n\nUnless... did we draw B4 or B5 back later? No such draw recorded.\n\nMore likely: **The state information contains an error in hand composition**.\n\nBut assuming the provided state is correct, perhaps the [B4,B5,B6] meld was formed differently? But \"Chi B5\" confirms we used B5 as the claimed tile? No \u2014 clarification: In \"Chi B5\", the notation means we completed the chow sequence that includes B5 \u2014 specifically, the chow is either [B3,B4,B5], [B4,B5,B6], or [B5,B6,B7].\n\nSince we played B7 right after, and the meld is [B4,B5,B6], it's most likely we held B4 and B6, claimed B5, formed [B4,B5,B6], then discarded B7.\n\nThus, after that action, we should **no longer have B4 or B6** in hand.\n\nTherefore, current hand should **not include B4 or B5** \u2014 unless we drew them again.\n\nBut no draw recorded between Chi B5 and now.\n\nThis suggests the **current hand listing is incorrect** or includes tiles already melded.\n\nBest resolution: Trust the meld list and history over the hand list.\n\nFrom history:\n- After Chi B5 and Play B7, our hand should have lost B4 and B6.\n- We had earlier Peng T5 (formed [T5,T5,T5]), so we added a T5 from discard.\n- Prior hand: before Chi B5, we had B4, B6 (to contribute), and after forming meld, we lose those.\n- Then we\u2019ve drawn tiles? Not specified, but wall exists.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the hand list reflects post-draw current state.\n\nBut given the confusion, let\u2019s assume the **provided hand is correct**: we currently hold [W3:2, B3:1, B4:1, B5:1], and we have a meld [B4,B5,B6] \u2014 this implies we have two B4s and two B5s: one copy used in meld, one in hand.\n\nIs that possible?\n\nTotal B4 tiles: 4. Unshown: 2. So 2 already revealed (discarded or melded). Previous player has [B3,B4,B5] \u2014 used one B4. We have [B4,B5,B6] \u2014 used one B4. So two B4s exposed. Unshown: 2 \u2014 matches. We can have one B4 in hand.\n\nSimilarly for B5: previous player used one in [B3,B4,B5], we used one in [B4,B5,B6], so two used. Unshown: 2. We can have one B5 in hand.\n\nSo yes, possible: we have extra B4 and B5 in hand.\n\nSo current live hand: W3, W3, B3, B4, B5.\n\nMelds: [T1,T2,T3], [T5,T5,T5], [B4,B5,B6].\n\nWait \u2014 but if we used B4 and B5 in the meld [B4,B5,B6], then the ones in hand are separate copies.\n\nSo total:\n- B4: two copies \u2014 one in meld, one in hand\n- B5: same\n\nBut a chow requires three consecutive tiles \u2014 so [B4,B5,B6] is one of each. So we used one B4, one B5, one B6 from our hand to form it via chi (we had B4 and B6, claimed B5).\n\nAfter that, we should not have B4 or B6 anymore \u2014 unless we drew another.\n\nBut we have B4 and B5 in hand now \u2014 so we must have drawn B4 and B5 after.\n\nNo record of drawing, but implied we did.\n\nSo plausible.\n\nThus, current hand: W3, W3, B3, B4, B5.\n\nWe need to discard one.\n\nNow evaluate patterns:\n\n- We have one pung: [T5,T5,T5]\n- Two chows: [T1,T2,T3], [B4,B5,B6]\n- Pair: W3,W3\n- Singles: B3, B4, B5\n\nPotential:\n- If we keep B3, B4, B5, we could aim for a second chow: [B3,B4,B5] \u2014 but we already used B4 and B5 in another meld? No 