e.\n\nSo next player discarded one B5.\n\nTotal: you (2), previous (1), next (1 discard) \u2192 4 \u2192 all accounted for.\n\nSo B5 is exhausted.\n\nSimilarly, B4: unshown: 1\n\nYou: two [B4,B5,B6] \u2192 2 B4\n\nPrevious: [B3,B4,B5] \u2192 1 B4\n\nTotal: 3 \u2192 should be 4 \u2192 one left unshown \u2192 yes, unshown: B4:1 \u2192 ok.\n\nBut B5: all 4 used \u2192 ok.\n\nNow, back to strategy.\n\nYour current hand cannot win immediately.\n\nYou must discard one.\n\nDiscarding T8 \u2192 leaves you waiting on F2 \u2192 impossible \u2192 dead hand.\n\nDiscarding F2 \u2192 leaves you waiting on T8 \u2192 live.\n\nEven though current expected score is only ~4 points, maybe more patterns emerge.\n\nWait \u2014 you have two identical chows [B4,B5,B6] \u2192 **Pure Double Chow** (1)\n\nAlso, you have a pung of T5 \u2014 T5 is simple \u2192 no bonus.\n\nBut do you have **All Simples**? All tiles are number 2\u20138, no 1,9 or honors?\n\nCheck:\n- [T1,T2,T3]: T1 and T3 are terminals! T1 and T3 \u2192 so has terminals \u2192 not All Simples.\n\nT1 is in [T1,T2,T3] \u2192 terminal \u2192 so hand contains terminals \u2192 cannot have All Simples or No Honors? Wait \u2014 No Honors means no honor tiles \u2014 you can have terminals.\n\nNo Honors: \"A hand with no honor tiles.\" \u2192 yes \u2014 if you discard F2, your entire hand is suited tiles \u2192 so **No Honors** (1 point) applies.\n\nBut you have T1 and T3 \u2192 terminals \u2192 so not All Simples.\n\nBut you do have **One Voided Suit** (missing Characters/W) \u2192 1 point.\n\nAnd **Single Wait** \u2192 1 point.\n\nAnd **Pure Double Chow** \u2192 1 point.\n\nStill only 4.\n\nBut wait \u2014 is there **Short Straight**? Two chows in same suit with successive numbers?\n\nYou have [B4,B5,B6] and [B4,B5,B6] \u2014 same, not successive.\n\nNo other chows in B.\n\nIn T: [T1,T2,T3] \u2014 only one.\n\nSo no Short Straight.\n\nAlternatively, could you have considered a different strategy earlier? But now it's too late.\n\nPerhaps you can win by other patterns if the hand develops, but right now, only hope is to discard F2 and wait for T8.\n\nMoreover, F2 is completely safe (all copies out), so discarding it won't feed anyone.\n\nT8 is wide open \u2014 4 copies in wall \u2014 someone might be waiting on it.\n\nBut you need it.\n\nAnother consideration: the player across has a concealed kong \u2014 likely W2 (since they BuGang W2). They are building something big.\n\nNext player has Kong of J1 \u2014 big hand.\n\nBut you are not in a position to rob kong unless you are one tile away.\n\nCurrently, you are one tile away only if you discard F2.\n\nIf you discard T8, you cannot win.\n\nTherefore, only rational choice is to discard F2 and hope to draw T8.\n\nBut even if you win, only 4 points \u2014 less than 8 \u2014 **cannot win at all**!\n\nCritical issue: **minimum 8 points required to win**.\n\nWith current composition, maximum possible score:\n\nLet\u2019s reevaluate patterns:\n\n- **Pure Double Chow**: 1\n- **No Honors**: 1 (after discarding F2)\n- **One Voided Suit**: 1\n- **Single Wait**: 1\n- **All Pungs**: No \u2014 you have chows\n- **Half Flush**: No \u2014 two suits\n- **Mixed Double Pung**: No pungs except T5\n- **T5 pung**: simple \u2192 no\n- **Two Melded Kongs**: No kong\n- **Melded Kong**: No\n- **Tile Hog**: No four identical tiles\n- **Self Drawn**: If you draw T8 \u2192 1 point\n- But still only 5 max (if Self Drawn) \u2192 still <8\n\nYou cannot reach 8 points.\n\nBut wait \u2014 you have **two** instances of [B4,B5,B6] \u2014 is that worth more than one Pure Double Chow?\n\nRule: \"Pure Double Chow: A hand with two identical chows. This pattern can be counted more than once if there are multiple melds meeting this requirement.\"\n\nBut here, you have two identical chows \u2014 that\u2019s one instance of \u201ctwo identical chows\u201d. It doesn\u2019t say \u201ceach additional chow\u201d, so likely only **once**.\n\nEven if counted twice (e.g., chow A and B are identical, chow A and C, etc.), but you have only two such chows \u2014 so one pair.\n\nSo only 1 point.\n\nConclusion: **this hand cannot qualify for any 8-point combination**.\n\nBut you must play.\n\nThe only logical move is to discard the **safe tile** to avoid feeding opponents.\n\nF2: all four copies already out \u2192 **completely safe** \u2192 cannot help anyone.\n\nT8: all four in wall \u2192 live tile \u2192 someone may be waiting on it.\n\nTherefore, **discard F2** to avoid risk.\n\nEven if you can't win, you shouldn't give others a chance.\n\nMoreover, by discarding F2, you at least keep a slim chance: if somehow more patterns apply.\n\nWait \u2014 do you have **Three Color Straight**? No \u2014 not listed in MCR.\n\nOr **Mixed Shifted Chows**? You have [T1,T2,T3] and [B4,B5,B6] \u2014 different suits, shifted by 3 \u2014 not consecutive.\n\nNot a recognized pattern.\n\nPerhaps **All Types**? Requires all three suits, winds, dragons \u2014 you lack dragon and Character suit.\n\nNo.\n\nSo indeed, hand is weak.\n\nBut wait \u2014 you have a pung of T5, and two chows in B: [B4,B5,B6]\u00d72.\n\nNo connection.\n\nFinal decision: **Play F2** is safer and keeps a (futile) chance to win; **Play T8** is dangerous and also futile.\n\nSince both lead to inability to win, choose the safer discard.\n\nReason: F2 is completely safe (no copies left), while T8 is fully available and could complete another player's hand.\n\nAnswer: Play F2\n\nBut let's confirm the valid actions: [Play F2; Play T8] \u2014 yes.\n\nEven though you can't win, you must act.\n\nBest to discard safely.\n\nEvaluation: Both possible discards lead to a hand that cannot reach the minimum 8 points required to win. However, discarding F2 is completely safe since all four F2 tiles are already exposed (in play or discarded), whereas T8 is still fully available and could potentially complete an opponent's winning hand.\n\nReason: To minimize risk to other players and follow safe discard principles, F2 should be discarded despite the hand's inability to win.\n\nAnswer: Play F2"}
